  "output": "Three approaches, ordered from exact to approximate:\n\n## 1. Exact — Branch-and-Bound / Branch-and-Cut (MILP)\nSolve the formulation from `formulation.md` directly with a MILP solver (CBC, HiGHS, or OR-Tools CP-SAT). The solver branches on the binary x_ij variables, using the LP relaxation as a bound; cuts strengthen it. MTZ handles subtours+capacity, and for the multi-trip version I'd add a trip index t ∈ {1,2}.\n\n- **Pros:** certified optimal (27 here), scales fine for this size\n- **Cons:** exponential worst-case runtime — VRP is NP-hard, so this dies beyond ~30–50 customers\n\n## 2. Heuristic — Clarke-Wright Savings + 2-opt\nClassic two-stage construction/improvement:\n1. Start with every customer on its own depot round trip: D→A→D, D→B→D, D→C→D = 38\n2. Merge routes whose endpoints save the most distance, if capacity allows: merging A–B saves 5, A–C saves 4, B–C saves 3 → merge {A,B} first (load 7 > 6, blocked!), so the **first feasible merge is {B,C}** (load exactly 6, saves 3) → D→B→C→D = 17, leaving D→A→D = 10\n3. Optionally polish each route with 2-opt segment reversals (no change here — routes are already optimal)\n\n- **Result: 27 = optimal**, reached in seconds with no solver dependency\n- **Pros:** fast, simple, no libraries; **Cons:** no optimality guarantee on bigger instances\n\n## 3. Simulation-based — Monte Carlo with stochastic travel times\nReplace fixed distances with sampled ones (e.g. d_ij ~ lognormal, mean = given distance, σ ≈ 20%), then run thousands of policy simulations:\n- **Policy A:** fixed plan D→{B,C} then D→{A}, rescheduling mid-route if a delay breaks feasibility\n- **Policy B:** greedy \"nearest feasible customer next\" with dynamic reload decisions\n\nCompare **P95 total distance and lateness** across policies — this answers \"which plan is *robust*?\" rather than \"which is shortest on paper?\" Pure Python (the allowed toolset has no numpy), so I'd sample ~1,000 scenarios for a usable confidence interval.\n\n---\n\n**My recommendation:** approaches **1 + 2 together** — savings gives a fast incumbent, MILP proves optimality. For this instance both land on 27, which is strong mutual validation. The simulation is worth adding only if travel-time variability actually matters for your team.\n\nWhich one should I implement?",
